      <h2>How to Document Your Income as Self-Employed</h2>
      <p>Unlike employees who submit a single payslip or employment contract, self-employed applicants must compile a fuller financial picture. Here's what Spanish immigration expects to see:</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Tax Returns (Most Important)</h3>
    <p>Your last <strong>1–2 years of personal tax returns</strong> are the foundation of your income documentation. In the US, that's your 1040; in the UK, your Self Assessment Tax Return; in Australia, your Tax Return. These show your declared income and are already verified by your tax authority.</p>
    <p>If you're in a country with a self-employed tax system (like Spain's RENTA form or the US Schedule C), provide that. This is your primary proof of income and income stability.</p>

    <h3>Invoices and Client Contracts</h3>
    <p>Compile <strong>12 months of invoices</strong> (or business activity statements) showing work delivered to foreign clients. These should:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Be dated and issued in your name or business name</li>
      <li>Show client names, invoice amounts, and payment terms</li>
      <li>Span at least 12 months to demonstrate consistency</li>
      <li>Total at or above the minimum income threshold</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you have formal client contracts, include a sample contract (even a generic one) that shows the scope of work, rates, and that the relationship is ongoing. This strengthens your application by showing you have real, structured business relationships, not ad-hoc project work.</p>

    <h3>Bank Statements</h3>
    <p>Submit <strong>6–12 months of business or personal bank statements</strong> showing deposits from clients. These corroborate your invoices—immigration wants to verify that clients actually paid you. Highlight incoming transfers from your clients and explain the currency if payments come in multiple currencies.</p>

    <h3>Accountant or Tax Professional Letter</h3>
    <p>If you work with an accountant or tax advisor (highly recommended), request a <strong>letter confirming your self-employed status, income history, and tax compliance</strong>. A sentence like "I confirm that [Your Name] is a self-employed professional with an average annual income of €[X] over the past [X] years" carries weight with immigration officers.</p>

    <h3>Handling Inconsistent or Variable Income</h3>
    <p>Self-employed income fluctuates—this is normal and expected. If your income varies month to month:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Average over 12 months</strong>: Calculate your average monthly income over the past year. If you average €3,000/month but earned €1,500 in a slow month and €5,000 in a strong month, that's fine.</li>
      <li><strong>Document the variation</strong>: Explain seasonal patterns (e.g., "My business peaks in Q4"). This shows understanding of your own business, not lack of stability.</li>
      <li><strong>Use a longer history if recent</strong>: If you only recently crossed the income threshold, provide 24 months of history if possible to show you're not a one-month spike.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Multiple Currencies</h3>
    <p>If you invoice in USD, GBP, EUR, or other currencies, <strong>convert to EUR</strong> using the average exchange rate for the period or the rate on the invoice date. Include both the original amount and EUR equivalent in your documentation. Use consistent conversion rates.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Autónomo Registration in Spain -->
<section class="msv__section">
  <div class="msv__container--narrow">
    <div class="msv__section-hd">
      <h2>Autónomo Registration in Spain (After Visa Approval)</h2>
      <p>One of the most common questions: Do I need to be registered as autónomo before applying for the DNV? The answer is <strong>no</strong>—but you'll likely need to register once you move to Spain. Here's the full breakdown:</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What is Autónomo Status?</h3>
    <p>An "autónomo" (self-employed professional) is someone who works for themselves and is registered with Spain's Social Security system (Seguridad Social). It's not a business entity—rather, it's a legal status that makes you eligible for Spanish social benefits, tax deductions, and liability protection.</p>
    <p>Spain requires self-employed people to register as autónomo for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Social security contributions (healthcare, unemployment, retirement)</li>
      <li>Tax filing and quarterly VAT (IVA) declarations</li>
      <li>Official business registration in the commerce registry</li>
      <li>Legal liability protection (in some cases)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Do You Need Autónomo Before Applying for the DNV?</h3>
    <p><strong>No.</strong> You do not need to be registered as autónomo to apply for the DNV. You apply based on your current self-employed status in your home country. Spanish immigration reviews your income documentation, invoices, and tax returns—not your autónomo registration status.</p>
    <p>Many applicants are surprised by this. You can be a self-employed consultant in the US, the UK, or Australia, with zero Spanish business registration, and still qualify for the DNV.</p>

    <h3>When Do You Register as Autónomo?</h3>
    <p>You register <strong>after your DNV visa is approved and you're in Spain</strong>. The timeline typically looks like this:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Before moving to Spain</strong>: Gather income docs, submit DNV application</li>
      <li><strong>Visa approved</strong>: Receive your DNV visa and move to Spain</li>
      <li><strong>Within 30 days of moving to Spain</strong>: Register as autónomo (or have your gestoría/accountant do it)</li>
      <li><strong>From that point</strong>: File quarterly VAT, annual tax return, pay social security contributions</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Autónomo Costs and Contributions</h3>
    <p><strong>First year (quota reducida—reduced rate):</strong> Approximately €80–100/month for new autónomos (those who've been registered for less than 24 months). This covers social security contributions.</p>
    <p><strong>Full rate (after 24 months):</strong> Approximately €300–400+/month depending on your activity category and income. This increases with your declared income.</p>
    <p>These contributions are separate from income tax (IRPF) and VAT (IVA). See the tax section below for a full breakdown.</p>

    <h3>Quarterly Tax Filings and VAT</h3>
    <p>Once registered as autónomo, you must file quarterly VAT (IVA) returns, even if you have €0 in Spanish sales. You'll declare:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Invoices issued (output VAT—what you charged clients)</li>
      <li>Deductible expenses (input VAT—business costs)</li>
      <li>Net VAT owed or credit</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Most self-employed professionals use a gestoría (tax agent) to handle this—it costs €40–100/month but saves significant administrative headache. Many foreigners find this essential for navigating Spanish bureaucracy correctly.</p>

    <h3>Can You Skip Autónomo Registration?</h3>
    <p><strong>Not legally.</strong> If you work as self-employed in Spain, you must register. Working without registration is considered black market ("economía sumergida") and can result in fines, back taxes, and legal complications. The registration is straightforward, the cost is manageable, and having proper status protects you.</p>

    <p><strong>Note:</strong> You don't need to register as autónomo to <em>apply</em> for the DNV—only to <em>legally work</em> in Spain once your visa is approved. Plan to register within your first 30 days of arrival.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Tax Implications for Self-Employed DNV Holders -->
<section class="msv__section--cream">
  <div class="msv__container--narrow">
    <div class="msv__section-hd">
      <h2>Tax Implications for Self-Employed DNV Holders</h2>
      <p>Understanding your tax obligations is critical to staying compliant and managing your finances in Spain. The tax situation for self-employed DNV holders is different from employees, especially regarding incentive programs.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>The Beckham Law (Ley de Beckham): Not Available for Self-Employed</h3>
    <p>Many relocating professionals ask about the Beckham Law, which offers significant tax breaks (flat 24% rate instead of progressive rates up to 45%) for qualifying non-residents. However, <strong>the Beckham Law generally does NOT apply to self-employed individuals</strong>—it's reserved for employees and high-income earners in specific contracts.</p>
    <p>If you're self-employed on the DNV, you don't qualify for this incentive. You'll pay standard Spanish progressive income tax. Plan accordingly.</p>
    <p><strong>Exception:</strong> If you transition from DNV self-employed to employing yourself via a Spanish company (and meet very specific criteria), you might explore different structures. Discuss this with a Spanish tax advisor.</p>

    <h3>Standard Spanish Progressive Tax Rates</h3>
    <p>As a self-employed professional in Spain, you're subject to Spain's income tax (IRPF—Impuesto sobre la Renta de las Personas Físicas). Current rates are:</p>
    <table class="msv__comparison-table">
      <tr>
        <th>Annual Income (EUR)</th>
        <th>Tax Rate</th>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>€0–€18,000</td>
        <td>19%</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>€18,001–€35,200</td>
        <td>24%</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>€35,201–€60,000</td>
        <td>30%</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>€60,001–€300,000</td>
        <td>37%</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>€300,001+</td>
        <td>45%</td>
      </tr>
    </table>
    <p><em>Note: These rates change annually. Consult a Spanish tax advisor for 2026 rates and regional variations.</em></p>
    <p>These are marginal rates, meaning you don't pay the highest rate on all income—only on the portion above each threshold. A self-employed professional earning €50,000/year in Spain would pay roughly 25–30% effective tax (not 37% on all income).</p>

    <h3>Deductible Business Expenses</h3>
    <p>As self-employed, you can deduct legitimate business expenses from your taxable income:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Office/workspace</strong>: Home office allowance, co-working space rentals</li>
      <li><strong>Software and subscriptions</strong>: Project management tools, Adobe, Microsoft, etc.</li>
      <li><strong>Equipment</strong>: Laptop, monitor, phone, office furniture (depreciated)</li>
      <li><strong>Professional development</strong>: Courses, certifications, books</li>
      <li><strong>Travel and meals</strong>: Client visits, business travel (within limits)</li>
      <li><strong>Accounting and legal</strong>: Gestoría, accountant, lawyer fees</li>
      <li><strong>Autónomo contributions</strong>: Social security payments (deductible)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Keep detailed receipts. Spanish tax authority is strict about deduction substantiation. Working with a gestoría ensures you claim valid deductions and avoid red flags.</p>

    <h3>Quarterly VAT (IVA) Obligations</h3>
    <p>Spain's VAT is 21% (standard rate) on goods and services. As a registered autónomo, you:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Charge VAT on invoices to Spanish customers (rare for remote self-employed, since most clients are abroad)</li>
      <li>Can reclaim VAT on business expenses (input VAT)</li>
      <li>File quarterly IVA returns showing net position</li>
      <li>If you work exclusively for non-Spanish clients and issue invoices outside Spain, VAT may not apply—discuss with your accountant</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Tax Residence and Double Taxation</h3>
    <p>Once you move to Spain under the DNV, you typically become a Spanish tax resident (spending 183+ days in Spain in a calendar year). This means:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You report worldwide income to Spain (not just Spanish income)</li>
      <li>You may still owe taxes in your home country on foreign-source income—check your home country's rules</li>
      <li>Spain has tax treaties with most countries to prevent double taxation</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you're a US citizen, for instance, you still owe US federal tax on worldwide income, even if living in Spain. However, the Foreign Earned Income Exclusion (FEIE) can reduce US tax. Consult a cross-border tax specialist if you have home country tax obligations.</p>

    <h3>Hiring a Gestoría (Highly Recommended)</h3>
    <p>Most self-employed foreigners in Spain hire a gestoría—a tax and administrative agent who handles:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Quarterly VAT filings</li>
      <li>Annual income tax return (Declaración de IRPF)</li>
      <li>Payroll (if you hire staff)</li>
      <li>Autónomo registration and contributions</li>
      <li>Compliance with Spanish regulations</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Cost:</strong> €40–100/month depending on complexity.</p>
    <p><strong>Benefit:</strong> Peace of mind, proper compliance, and access to a professional who understands Spanish tax law. For most foreigners, this is money well spent.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Common Mistakes Self-Employed Applicants Make -->
<section class="msv__section">
  <div class="msv__container--narrow">
    <div class="msv__section-hd">
      <h2>Common Mistakes Self-Employed Applicants Make</h2>
      <p>We've reviewed hundreds of DNV applications. Here are the mistakes that most often lead to rejections or delays. Avoid these and strengthen your application.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="msv__steps">
      <div class="msv__step">
        <div class="msv__step-num">1</div>
        <div class="msv__step-body">
          <h3>Inconsistent Invoice Records</h3>
          <p>Submitting invoices with typos, inconsistent client names, or gaps in invoicing. If you invoice "John Smith" in January and "John Smith Consulting Inc." in March, it raises red flags. Spanish immigration wants to see consistent, professional documentation. Clean up your records before applying. Use standardized invoice templates with your name, client, date, and amount clearly stated.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__step">
        <div class="msv__step-num">2</div>
        <div class="msv__step-body">
          <h3>No Formal Client Contracts</h3>
          <p>Relying only on email exchanges or verbal agreements. If you have formal contracts with clients—even simple one-pagers—include them. They show you have real, structured business relationships. If you don't have written contracts, create one for your main clients before applying. This protects you and strengthens your visa application.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__step">
        <div class="msv__step-num">3</div>
        <div class="msv__step-body">
          <h3>Income Below the Threshold</h3>
          <p>Submitting documentation that shows you're below the €2,850/month minimum. Double-check your calculations. If you're slightly below, wait until you've built up a month or two above the threshold to apply. The application will likely be rejected if you're underthreshold, and you can't reapply immediately.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__step">
        <div class="msv__step-num">4</div>
        <div class="msv__step-body">
          <h3>Too Much Spanish Income</h3>
          <p>Exceeding the 20% Spanish income cap or having significant income from Spain. If more than 20% of your annual income comes from Spanish clients, you may fail the "foreign income" requirement. Review your client list before applying. If you have Spanish clients, plan to reduce that portion or explain the temporary nature of those contracts.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__step">
        <div class="msv__step-num">5</div>
        <div class="msv__step-body">
          <h3>Registering as Autónomo Before Approval</h3>
          <p>Getting registered as autónomo in Spain before your DNV is approved. This can actually hurt your application because immigration may question whether you're working without a valid work visa. Wait until your visa is approved. You have time to register after you arrive. Some consultants mistakenly advise this—ignore that advice.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__step">
        <div class="msv__step-num">6</div>
        <div class="msv__step-body">
          <h3>Poor-Quality Documentation</h3>
          <p>Submitting blurry scans, photocopies of photocopies, or unverified documents. Take clear, high-resolution photos or scans of all documents. Include certified translations if your documents are in a language other than Spanish or English. Professional presentation matters—it signals you take your application seriously.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__step">
        <div class="msv__step-num">7</div>
        <div class="msv__step-body">
          <h3>Single-Client Dependency</h3>
          <p>100% of income from one client. While not explicitly prohibited, this weakens your application and increases rejection risk. Before applying, aim to have at least 2–3 main clients or supplementary work that diversifies your income sources. This also reduces your business risk if a client leaves.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__step">
        <div class="msv__step-num">8</div>
        <div class="msv__step-body">
          <h3>Mixing Personal and Business Finance</h3>
          <p>No separation between personal and business bank accounts, making it hard to track income. Open a dedicated business or freelance account if you don't have one. This clarifies your income, makes tax filing easier, and strengthens your application by showing organized finances.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__step">
        <div class="msv__step-num">9</div>
        <div class="msv__step-body">
          <h3>Missing Health Insurance</h3>
          <p>Forgetting to include proof of health insurance valid in Spain. This is a visa requirement. Arrange insurance before submitting your application—it's one of the easier documents to provide and a common rejection reason if omitted.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

